.TH RUN-APPSCAN 1 "2026-10-12" "cti-agent 1.3" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
run-appscan \- weekly application-security report to the App Dev audience, and scanner-login faults to the operator
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B run\-appscan
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-to\-dev ]
.PP
.B sudo cti\-agent run\-appscan
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-to\-dev ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B run-appscan
renders the week's Qualys WAS scan results with
.BR cti\-appscan (1)
and mails them through
.BR cti\-mailer (1)
.BR "\-\-lane was" ,
to the people who own application code rather than the digest audience.
No model is involved.
.PP
It is run by
.B cti\-agent\-appscan.service
from
.B cti\-agent\-appscan.timer
every Monday at 07:30
.RB ( Persistent=true ),
half an hour after the weekly rollup. The unit runs as
.BR ctiagent ,
with
.B cti\-agent\-alert@.service
on failure.
.PP
The steps, in order:
.TP
.B 1. Prepare
Checks that
.I $FLEET_CODE/bin/cti\-appscan
and
.I $FLEET_CODE/bin/cti\-mailer
are executable (fatal if not), sets
.I $FLEET_HOME/state
to 0700, creates today's HTML and text files empty at 0600 before anything
is written to them, and deletes today's authentication-failure file, whose
absence after the run is itself a signal.
.TP
.B 2. Report
.B cti\-appscan \-\-out \-\-text\-out \-\-auth\-fail\-out
reads the scan notifications in the CTI mailbox and the WAS findings API and
writes the report. Fatal on a non-zero exit.
.TP
.B 3. Subject
.B cti\-appscan \-\-subject\-only
is run as a second, separate collection. Fatal if it fails or prints
nothing.
.TP
.B 4. Send
.B cti\-mailer \-\-lane was
with the HTML, text and subject, plus
.B \-\-dry\-run
on a dry run. The lane resolves
.B WAS_TO
against
.B WAS_ALLOW_TO
and refuses if either is unset; it never falls back to the digest audience.
Fatal on failure.
.TP
.B 5. Scanner logins (non-fatal)
After the report has gone: if the authentication-failure file is absent,
the binary is older than
.B \-\-auth\-fail\-out
and the runner says nothing was checked. If it is empty, every configured
scanner credential worked. If it has lines, the applications are logged
and, on a real run,
.B cti\-alert \-\-unit cti\-agent\-appscan.service \-\-kind HOLD
mails the operator naming them and the file. A missing or failing
.B cti\-alert
is logged; the lane still succeeds.
.PP
A dry run still reads the mailbox and the scanner API and overwrites
today's report files. It sends nothing through
.BR cti\-mailer ,
and it logs, but does not alert on, authentication failures.
.SH OPTIONS
Shell arguments, in any order.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Render, print the subject, and pass
.B \-\-dry\-run
to the mailer; no alert is sent.
.TP
.B \-\-to\-dev
A test send: the report goes through
.B cti\-mailer \-\-to\-dev
to
.B FLEET_DEV_EMAIL
only, subject prefixed
.BR "[DEV TEST]" .
.B WAS_TO
and
.B WAS_ALLOW_TO
are not consulted, so the report can be seen before the lane's audience is
configured. An authentication-failure alert goes through
.B cti\-alert \-\-to\-dev
to the same address. With
.B \-\-dry\-run
as well, nothing is mailed.
.PP
Anything else is refused with exit status 1.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.I fleet.env
is sourced with every assignment exported, and its values replace the
process environment, except
.B FLEET_HOME
and
.BR FLEET_CODE ,
which keep the value the unit or wrapper injected. The Go binaries read
their credentials only from the environment, so this is how they arrive.
.TP
.B FLEET_HOME
State directory.
Default:\~\fB$HOME\fR (not \fI$HOME/fleet\fR); the unit sets
\fI/var/lib/cti\-agent\fR.
.TP
.B FLEET_CODE
Code directory.
Default:\~\fB$FLEET_HOME\fR; the unit sets \fI/opt/cti\-agent\fR.
.TP
.B FLEET_ENV
The config file.
Default:\~\fI$FLEET_HOME/fleet.env\fR.
.TP
.BR WAS_TO ", " WAS_ALLOW_TO
The audience and its allowlist, read by
.B cti\-mailer
(section 4, "application security"). No fallback.
.TP
.B FLEET_OPERATOR_EMAIL
Where
.B cti\-alert
sends an authentication fault (section 4).
.PP
Graph and Qualys credentials (sections 1 and 2) are read by
.BR cti\-appscan (1).
.SH FILES
Under
.IR /var/lib/cti\-agent/state ,
directory mode 0700.
.I DATE
is the UTC date.
.TP
.IR appscan\-DATE.html ", " appscan\-DATE.txt
The report, 0600. A second run on the same day replaces them.
.TP
.I appscan\-authfail\-DATE.tsv
One line per scan whose configured credential failed, application first;
written 0600 by
.BR cti\-appscan .
Present and empty: checked, nothing failed. Absent: nothing was checked.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The report was sent (or rendered, on a dry run). Authentication failures
and alerting problems do not change this.
.TP
.B 1
An unknown or extra argument;
.B cti\-appscan
or
.B cti\-mailer
is missing;
.B cti\-appscan
failed or produced an empty subject; or the send failed, including the
mailer's refusal when
.B WAS_TO
or
.B WAS_ALLOW_TO
is unset.
.SH EXAMPLES
Render this week's report and check who it would go to:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent run\-appscan \-\-dry\-run
sudo less /var/lib/cti\-agent/state/appscan\-$(date \-u +%F).txt
.EE
.PP
See whether a scanner login failed:
.PP
.EX
sudo cat /var/lib/cti\-agent/state/appscan\-authfail\-$(date \-u +%F).tsv
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cti\-appscan (1),
.BR cti\-mailer (1),
.BR cti\-alert (1),
.BR cti\-agent (1)
.PP
RUNBOOK.md section 11f; fleet\-kit/README.md, "The weekly AppSec report".
